    "validation_file": {
      "id": "2",
      "name": "test.py",
      "contents": "import re\nfrom unittest import TestCase\nfrom unittest_runner import ValidationProtocol\n\n\ndef read_code():\n    with open('main.py') as main_file:\n        return main_file.read()\n\n\nclass TestBucketModify(TestCase):\n    @classmethod\n    def setUpClass(cls):\n        \"\"\"Run the student's code once and save the results\"\"\"\n        cls.log = ValidationProtocol().get_neighborhood_log()\n        cls.painter = cls.log.painter_logs[0] if cls.log.painter_logs else None\n\n    def test_keeps_the_bucket_check(self):\n        \"\"\"Your program still checks is_on_bucket()\"\"\"\n        if not re.search(r'is_on_bucket\\s*\\(\\s*\\)', read_code()):\n            self.fail(\"Keep the if is_on_bucket(): check from the starter code. The else goes right after it.\")\n\n    def test_uses_else(self):\n        \"\"\"Your program uses an else branch\"\"\"\n        if not re.search(r'^\\s*else\\s*:', read_code(), re.MULTILINE):\n            self.fail(\"This level asks you to add an else branch. Add else: after your if, with an indented line that tells the Painter what to do.\")\n\n    def test_painter_did_something(self):\n        \"\"\"Your Painter moves or turns when it isn't on a bucket\"\"\"\n        if self.painter is None:\n            self.skipTest(\"Your code didn't run far enough to check yet. Make sure the starter code is still there and run again.\")\n        p = self.painter\n        start = (p.starting_position.x, p.starting_position.y, p.starting_position.direction)\n        end = (p.ending_position.x, p.ending_position.y, p.ending_position.direction)\n        self.assertNotEqual(\n            start,\n            end,\n            \"Right now your Painter ends where it started, facing the same way. Add an else branch that makes the Painter move or turn when it isn't on a bucket.\"\n        )",
      "folderId": "0",
      "active": false,
      "type": "validation"
    },
    "teacher_markdown": "Misconceptions and what to test for:\r\n\r\n- The Painter is not on a bucket at the start, so the if branch does not run and the else branch does. Ask: \"Which branch ran, and how do you know?\" That is objective 2 in a Painter context.\r\n- The starter looks like it does nothing. That is the point. With only an if, the False case is silent. The else is what makes the Painter act.\r\n- Forgetting the colon after else, or not indenting the line under it, is the most common error.\r\n- Else never takes a condition. Writing else not is_on_bucket(): is a syntax error.\r\n- Any move or turn is a good answer. Ask students why they chose theirs, for example \"I moved so the Painter would reach the bucket.\"\r\n- A second if not is_on_bucket(): works, but the level asks for an else. Ask: \"What are the two ways to write this?\"\r\n- If the Painter stood on a bucket, the if branch would run and the else would not. Only one path ever runs.\r\n- If a student uses Build Python, they still own the program. Ask them to explain which branch ran.\r\n\r\nValidation checks that the program keeps the is_on_bucket() check, uses an else, and ends in a different square or facing a different direction than it started."
  },
